<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Karauli State</h1><p class="st-meta">1348 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Karauli</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Jadaun Rajput state of eastern Rajasthan, whose rulers claimed descent from Krishna's Yadava clan and whose capital grew around the Madan Mohan temple.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadaun">Jadaun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajputs</a>, claiming descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a>, ruled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayana">Bayana</a> and Timangarh before the 14th century. In 1348 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjun-pal">Arjun Pal</a> founded the town of Karauli, which became the capital of the state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Karauli was a hilly, largely rural state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambal-river">Chambal</a> ravines. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> accepted Mughal overlordship and later paid tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>. The temple of Madan Mohan, a form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, made the town a pilgrimage centre, and red sandstone from local quarries was used for its palaces. In 1817 Karauli signed a treaty with the British.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Karauli joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/matsya-union">Matsya Union</a> in 1948, which merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in 1949. It is now a district of Rajasthan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/karauli-state.jpg" alt="Karauli State" width="320" height="266"><figcaption>Image: Imperial Gazetteer of India, 1909 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Karauli.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1348 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Karauli</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Arjun Pal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Braj Bhasha</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>India</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Karauli</dd></dl></aside></div>
